<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bharmal</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/1574">1574</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Raja Bharmal, Bihari Mal</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Bharmal was the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kachhwaha">Kachhwaha</a> Raja of Amber in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajasthan">Rajasthan</a> from 1548 until his death in 1574. In 1562 he became one of the first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajputs">Rajput</a> rulers to submit to the Mughal emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akbar">Akbar</a>, and gave him his daughter in marriage; she became the mother of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jahangir">Jahangir</a>. The alliance made the Kachhwahas leading Mughal nobles and laid the foundations of the later state of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jaipur">Jaipur</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bharmal.jpg" alt="Bharmal" width="320" height="471" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Raja_bharmal_painting.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Bharmal was the fourth son of Raja Prithviraj Singh I of Amer and Rani Apoorva Devi, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rathore">Rathore</a> princess of Bikaner. After Prithviraj's death in 1527 the throne passed to his eldest son Puranmal, who was killed at the Battle of Mandrail on 19 January 1534 while helping the Mughal emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/humayun">Humayun</a> recover the fort of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bayana">Bayana</a>. As Puranmal's son Sujamal was a minor, the throne went to his brother Bhim Singh and then to Bhim Singh's son Ratan Singh, who was killed by his half-brother Askaran. The nobles of Amer deposed Askaran and on 1 June 1548 chose Bharmal, then about fifty years old.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>In 1556 Bharmal helped the Mughal commandant Majnun Khan Qaqshal, who told <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akbar">Akbar</a> of it, and Akbar received Bharmal and his relatives at court with robes of honour. In 1562 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kachhwaha">Kachhwahas</a> were in danger when Mirza Muhammad Sharaf-ud-din Hussain, the Mughal governor of Mewat, took up the claim of Sujamal. He led a large army to Amber, drove the Kachhwahas into the forests and hills, and took Bharmal's son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jagannath">Jagannath</a> and two nephews as hostages for a fixed tribute.</p><p>Through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akbar">Akbar</a>'s courtier Chaghtai Khan, Bharmal met the emperor at his camp at Sanganer on 20 January 1562, while Akbar was on pilgrimage to the dargah of Khwaja Moinuddin Chisti at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ajmer">Ajmer</a>. There he offered his eldest daughter in marriage. At Sambhar the hostages were surrendered to Akbar, and on 6 February 1562 the marriage took place. Bharmal was then made commander of 5,000 cavalry, the highest rank open to a noble at the Mughal court, and his son Bhagwant Das and grandson Man Singh went with Akbar to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agra">Agra</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Bharmal died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agra">Agra</a> on 27 January 1574 and was succeeded by his eldest son, Raja Bhagwant Das. He had at least thirteen sons and four daughters. The chronicler Nizamuddin Ahmad counted him among the leading rajas of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hindustan">Hindustan</a> who entered the emperor's service, and the Tarikh-i-Salim praised his allegiance and courage. He, Bhagwant Das and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/man-singh-i">Man Singh I</a> each became the highest mansabdar of their times.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Bharmal” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Their rise began when <b>Raja Bharmal</b> allied with Akbar in 1562 and gave him his daughter in marriage.</blockquote><a href="/state/jaipur-state">Jaipur State</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Amber, in what is now <a href="/countries/india">India</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lahore">Lahore</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/pakistan">Pakistan</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=26.98,75.87&amp;year=1564" aria-label="Amber on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6547 1174 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Bharmal"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6997 1444h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6997 1444h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=26.98,75.87&amp;year=1564">What was here in 1564? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
